.TH SYSTEM_UPDATE 1 "April 2026" "version 3.1.10" "Ubuntu Server Maintenance Tool"
.SH NAME
system_update \- automated Ubuntu/Debian server maintenance and update utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B system_update
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B system_update
is a production-ready command-line utility for automated Ubuntu/Debian server maintenance. It provides safe, logged, and auditable system updates with multiple operation modes suitable for web servers, database servers, and production environments.
.PP
The tool offers three main update strategies: force update (with automatic reboot), all updates (without reboot), and critical security updates only. All operations are logged to /var/log/ubuntu_maintenance.log for audit trails.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Perform a full system update with automatic reboot scheduled in 5 minutes. This mode runs apt update, apt full-upgrade, apt autoremove, apt autoclean, and schedules a system reboot. The reboot can be cancelled with 'sudo shutdown -c' within the 5-minute window.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Perform a full system update without automatic reboot. This is the recommended mode for most production servers. Runs apt update, apt full-upgrade, apt autoremove, and apt autoclean. You control when to reboot.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-critical
Install only critical security updates. This conservative mode runs apt update and apt upgrade (not full-upgrade), installing only security patches. Best for minimal risk and downtime.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-info
Display comprehensive system information including hostname, kernel version, available updates, security updates, disk usage, uptime, and last reboot time. Does not require sudo privileges.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dry\-run
Preview mode that shows what commands would be executed without making any changes to the system. Always use this first in production environments. Must be combined with another option (e.g., -d -a).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help message with usage information and examples.
.SH INTERACTIVE MODE
When run without arguments,
.B system_update
presents a full-screen interactive menu with the following options:
.PP
.nf
Run Updates
Manage Schedule
System Information
View Logs
Help
Exit
.fi
.PP
Navigation uses the arrow keys and Enter. Escape or q returns to the previous screen.
.PP
.B Manage Schedule
provides an interactive interface to create, view, and remove automated update schedules using cron. Users can set up daily, weekly, or weekday schedules with their choice of update mode (all, critical, or force). This eliminates the need to manually edit crontab files.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
Failure (insufficient privileges, invalid arguments, or system errors)
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/log/ubuntu_maintenance.log
Primary log file for all operations (falls back to /tmp/ubuntu_maintenance.log if primary is unavailable)
.TP
.I /var/log/ubuntu_maintenance_verbose.log
Verbose command output log used by the in-app log viewer (falls back to /tmp/ubuntu_maintenance_verbose.log if needed)
.TP
.I /usr/bin/ubuntu-maintenance
Installed binary location
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B sudo system_update
Run in interactive mode with menu
.TP
.B sudo system_update \-\-dry\-run \-a
Preview all updates without applying them (recommended first step)
.TP
.B sudo system_update \-a
Apply all updates without rebooting (recommended for production)
.TP
.B sudo system_update \-c
Apply only critical security updates
.TP
.B sudo system_update \-f
Full update with automatic reboot in 5 minutes
.TP
.B system_update \-i
View system information (no sudo required)
.SH CRON AUTOMATION
For automated weekly updates without reboot:
.PP
.nf
sudo crontab -e
# Add:
0 3 * * 0 /usr/bin/ubuntu-maintenance -a >> /var/log/automated_updates.log 2>&1
.fi
.PP
For daily security updates only:
.PP
.nf
0 2 * * * /usr/bin/ubuntu-maintenance -c >> /var/log/security_updates.log 2>&1
.fi
